WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:05.466
Welcome to Zonal. This first lesson takes
about two minutes.

00:00:05.816 --> 00:00:09.181
You will learn what Zonal does, the two
ideas it is built on,

00:00:09.181 --> 00:00:10.947
and how to make your first Snap.

00:00:12.340 --> 00:00:15.482
Look at this desktop. Windows open
wherever they like.

00:00:15.482 --> 00:00:17.750
They overlap, and they hide each other.

00:00:18.177 --> 00:00:20.863
Every time you want two of them side by
side,

00:00:20.863 --> 00:00:23.250
you drag corners until they roughly fit.

00:00:23.250 --> 00:00:24.980
Zonal does that part for you.

00:00:25.452 --> 00:00:30.162
Zonal's first idea is the Zone: an area of
your screen where a window goes.

00:00:30.162 --> 00:00:33.805
Here you can see three Zones, numbered
one, two and three.

00:00:34.166 --> 00:00:35.873
You choose the Zones once.

00:00:35.873 --> 00:00:40.993
After that, every window lands exactly in
place, with no gaps and no overlaps.

00:00:41.442 --> 00:00:46.091
The second idea is the Layout: a set of
Zones that covers a whole display.

00:00:46.091 --> 00:00:49.860
This one is called Priority Grid: a wide
Zone in the middle,

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:51.934
and two narrow ones at the sides.

00:00:52.435 --> 00:00:55.696
Zonal comes with five Layouts, and you can
make your own.

00:00:55.696 --> 00:00:57.528
Each display has its own Layout.

00:00:57.922 --> 00:01:01.260
The first time you open Zonal, this
welcome window appears.

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:03.014
It walks you through the setup.

00:01:03.432 --> 00:01:07.838
To move the windows of other apps, Zonal
needs Accessibility access.

00:01:07.838 --> 00:01:10.365
Click Open Settings, and turn Zonal on.

00:01:10.365 --> 00:01:13.151
When the window says Granted, you are
done.

00:01:13.151 --> 00:01:15.808
It is the only permission Zonal asks for.

00:01:16.524 --> 00:01:21.553
Zonal has no Dock icon. It lives in the
menu bar, at the top of the screen.

00:01:21.553 --> 00:01:23.700
Click its icon to open the menu.

00:01:24.299 --> 00:01:27.932
Here you choose the Layout of each
display, edit your Layouts,

00:01:27.932 --> 00:01:30.511
reopen the welcome window, or open
Settings.

00:01:30.995 --> 00:01:35.814
Now, your first Snap. Start dragging a
window by its title bar, and hold Shift.

00:01:35.814 --> 00:01:36.851
The Zones appear.

00:01:37.317 --> 00:01:42.256
Move over a Zone, and let go of the mouse
button. The window fills the Zone exactly.

00:01:42.256 --> 00:01:43.138
That is a Snap.

00:01:44.783 --> 00:01:49.256
Where does this help? Everywhere you work
with more than one window. A few examples.

00:01:49.606 --> 00:01:53.593
Writing a report: your sources on the
left, the document in the middle,

00:01:53.593 --> 00:01:54.941
your notes on the right.

00:01:55.291 --> 00:01:59.415
Coding: the editor in the big Zone, the
browser and a terminal beside it.

00:01:59.765 --> 00:02:04.694
On a video call: the call, its notes, and
the shared document, each in its own Zone,

00:02:04.694 --> 00:02:05.868
none hiding another.

00:02:07.418 --> 00:02:08.018
To recap.

00:02:08.018 --> 00:02:13.216
A Zone is where a window goes, and a
Layout is a set of Zones for one display.

00:02:13.216 --> 00:02:16.615
Zonal needs Accessibility access, and
nothing else.

00:02:16.615 --> 00:02:20.881
And to Snap, hold Shift while you drag,
then let go over a Zone.

00:02:22.431 --> 00:02:26.614
In the next lesson, you will learn
everything about Snapping with the mouse.

00:02:26.614 --> 00:02:29.036
All the lessons are also in the Zonal
guide.
